---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-vacation`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Vacation',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Vacation', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Vacation: How Much a Sunny Trip Actually Boosts Your Levels"
  description="A week in Cancun in February — real 25(OH)D impact, how long the boost lasts, the sunburn/melanoma trade-off, and why you still need supplementation at home."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-vacation", label: "Vitamin D & Vacation" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Vacation</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      A February beach week in Mexico, the Caribbean, or Southern Europe delivers a
      genuine but temporary vitamin D boost. The magnitude often surprises people — a
      few days of tropical sun can raise 25(OH)D by 10–20 ng/mL — but so does how
      quickly the boost fades once you're back home. Vacation is not a year-round
      vitamin D strategy.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Typical numbers</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Fair-skinned adult starting at 20 ng/mL (winter deficient) — a week of beach sun in the tropics with realistic exposure (2–3 hours daily, some sunscreen) typically raises 25(OH)D to 35–45 ng/mL over the following 2–3 weeks.</li>
      <li>Darker-skinned adult starting at 15 ng/mL — smaller boost, maybe to 25–30 ng/mL.</li>
      <li>The boost peaks 2–3 weeks after vacation return (lag reflects 25(OH)D pharmacokinetics).</li>
      <li>Serum 25(OH)D then declines with normal winter kinetics — ~1–2 ng/mL per month back at high latitude without sun or supplement.</li>
      <li>By 3–4 months post-vacation, you're back to baseline.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The reserves angle</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Some vitamin D from vacation exposure gets stored in adipose tissue and slowly
      released. See our <a href="/vitamin-d-reserves" class="text-amber-700 underline">reserves calculator</a>
      for a pharmacokinetic model. Adipose stores buffer the decline somewhat but don't
      prevent it. A single summer holiday won't carry you through the following winter.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The sunburn / melanoma trade-off</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Intermittent intense sun exposure — like a vacation from a temperate country to
      a tropical destination — is the single strongest recognised risk factor for
      melanoma, particularly if it produces sunburns. This is where the vitamin D
      trade-off becomes unfavourable: gaining 15 ng/mL of 25(OH)D on a vacation isn't
      worth a childhood sunburn or a serious melanoma risk factor. Sunscreen, hats,
      and gradual acclimation are essential.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical vacation vitamin D</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Enjoy the sunshine benefit — brief unprotected exposure (5–15 minutes, arms and legs) at the start of the day multiple times during the trip provides most of the vitamin D benefit with least burn risk.</li>
      <li>Then apply sunscreen for the rest of the day.</li>
      <li>Never seek sunburn to boost vitamin D — it doesn't work faster, and the cancer risk is real.</li>
      <li>Continue supplementation after the vacation — the boost fades within months.</li>
      <li>If you tend toward winter deficiency, one tropical vacation isn't the answer; year-round supplementation is.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Northern European "sun holiday" pattern</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Cross-sectional data from UK, Danish, and Swedish cohorts show adults who take
      annual Mediterranean holidays have modestly higher year-round 25(OH)D than
      non-travellers. The effect is measurable but modest — 3–5 ng/mL average
      annual difference. Individual variation is large.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Winter vacation strategy for high-latitude residents</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      A February beach week can meaningfully improve late-winter mood and 25(OH)D.
      But it doesn't replace supplementation. Northern European or Canadian adults who
      choose winter holidays for vitamin D benefit should still supplement year-round
      — treat the vacation as a bonus, not the strategy.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Tan preservation is not a vitamin D goal;
      neither is deliberate darkening of skin. Prioritise skin health.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-vacation" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
